			<div class="content" id="post-content-4283797">
									

<p>An absolutely excellent piece. You really exploit the loneliness and isolation of the Antarctic setting and create one of the best examples I've seen on-site of diary entries used for a more action-heavy, thriller-esque type of narrative. The juxaposition of the past and present elements is also really good; the ending especially transforms the curated, almost fictional-seeming diaries to something that feels much more real and direct.</p>
<p>Very easy +1. This should be read as an example of how to do exploratory/horror-from-time-based skips properly.</p>

